{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke all these words, saying.</b> He added לאמר to indicate that all these utterances were spoken in a single utterance — that which the mouth cannot speak and that which the ear cannot hear. And so they said in Midrash Sinai Rabbah: one verse says (Psalms 147:4) ״מונה מספר לכוכבים לכלם שמות יקרא״, and another verse says (Isaiah 40:26) ״המוציא במספר צבאם לכלם בשם יקרא״ — when the Holy One, blessed be He, calls them, He calls names to all of them at once." "<b>Or</b>, you may interpret לאמר as referring to the hidden: for all the words of HaShem, may He be exalted, contain revealed and hidden, and the דבור (speech) refers to the revealed and the אמירה (saying) refers to the hidden. And this is like the sense of ״וידבר ה׳ אל משה לאמר״ in many places in the Torah, for He told Moses the revealed and the hidden. And I have already mentioned this in the section of (Exodus 13:2) ״קדש לי״. Therefore He declared to Israel here that they were all prophets, and this is what is written (Psalms 62:12) ״אחת דבר אלהים שתים זו שמעתי״." "<b>And know</b> that the opinion of the Rambam, of blessed memory, concerning the standing at Mount Sinai in chapter 33 of the second part of his book, is that the mighty voice that Israel heard was created, and they heard it without division into letters, and they heard it only once, and this was in the first two commandments. And concerning this the Torah says (Deuteronomy 5:19) ״קול גדול ולא יסף״, and it is written (ibid.) ״כשמעכם את הקול״, and it is written (ibid. 4) ״קול דברים אתם שומעים״ — the sound of words, not actual words. And this is what is written ״בעבור ישמע העם בדברי עמך״, meaning: the people will hear the sound and the speech that is with you. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: אנכי and לא יהיה לך — from the mouth of the Almighty we heard them. And this is the difference between Moses and Israel: Israel heard the sound of words, while Moses heard the actual words. Therefore Moses would make them hear with division into letters, as it is written (ibid. 5) ״אנכי עומד בין ה׳ וביניכם בעת ההיא להגיד לכם את דבר ה׳״ — meaning the two first commandments which they grasped from Him but did not hear with division into letters. And for this reason Onkelos translated ״וידבר ה׳ אל משה לאמר״, and likewise ״וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה״, as ומליל ה׳ — and he translated according to the grasp of Israel, and ״אל ידבר עמנו אלהים״ as ולא יתמלל עמנא מן קדם ה׳ — and he did not say ולא ימלל עמנא, because the speech was not to them with division into letters but only hearing the sound alone. And after they heard the sound in the first two, their souls flew away, as it is written (Song of Songs 5:6) ״נפשי יצאה בדברו״, and they thought they would continue to hear the sound and were greatly afraid, and they said to Moses ״ועתה למה נמות״ etc., ״קרב אתה ושמע״ etc., ״דבר אתה עמנו ונשמעה״ etc. And Moses would hear all the commandments one by one and make them hear them, and this is what is written ״משה ידבר והאלהים יעננו בקול״. You find, then, that in the first two Israel heard the sound just as Moses did, except that Israel heard it without division into letters and Moses with division into letters; but the remaining commandments they did not hear at all from the mouth of HaShem, and they received them from the side of the prophecy of Moses like the rest of the commandments. And so they said at the beginning of Shir HaShirim Rabbah: (Deuteronomy 33:4) ״תורה צוה לנו משה״ — מצות כמנין תור״ה — אנכי ולא יהיה לך מפי הגבורה שמעום — for through hearing a single sound they grasped the two commandments, and this is what is written (Psalms 62:12) ״אחת דבר אלהים שתים זו שמעתי״. This is the opinion of the Moreh ha-Nevukhim, of blessed memory." "<b>But</b> the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, is that all Ten Commandments Israel heard from the mouth of HaShem. And this is the sense of ״וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה״, and likewise Moses mentioned in Mishneh Torah ״את הדברים האלה דבר ה׳ אל כל קהלכם״, and it is written ״ויכתבם על שני לוחות אבנים״ — we learn that just as He said ״אל כל קהלכם״, so He wrote them on the tablets. And as for what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said — אנכי ולא יהיה לך מפי הגבורה שמעום — from which it would be understood that the remaining commandments they did not hear from the mouth of the Almighty: the truth is that the word שמיעה includes hearing of the ear, as it is written (Proverbs 15:31) ״אזן שומעת תוכחת חיים״, and it also includes understanding of the heart, as it is written (I Kings 3:9) ״לב שומע״. And Israel did not have hearing and understanding from the mouth of the Almighty except in the first two; and the rest they did hear from the mouth of HaShem, but they did not understand them, and therefore Moses needed to make them understand. This is what he said (Deuteronomy 5) ״אנכי עומד בין ה׳ וביניכם בעת ההיא להגיד לכם את דבר ה׳״. And this is the meaning of the verse ״משה ידבר והאלהים יעננו בקול״ — that is, what Elohim would answer in voice, Moses would speak to Israel. And the term עניה here means beginning, as in (Job 3:2) ״ויען איוב״ — an answering that precedes speech. Thus did he, of blessed memory, write these things." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> וידבר אלהים — this is its meaning: וידבר ה׳, but He mentioned אלהים with regard to the grasp of Israel, and it is the word of the prophet, peace be upon him, (Habakkuk 3:10) ״נתן תהום קולו״, and concerning this it was mentioned above ״ומשה עלה אל האלהים״. And for this reason they said: אנכי ולא יהיה לך — מפי הגבורה שמענו אותם. And therefore Onkelos translated in his wondrous wisdom: in every instance of the name אלהים in this section, he renders it ד׳ or מימרא דה׳ — not to obscure but to explain that it is the Glory together with the Special Name that is speaking in it. And in every place in the section where the Special Name is mentioned, he does not designate it and does not translate it as יקרא or מימרא at all, as is his practice in other places of the Torah; rather he translated ״וירד ה׳ על הר סיני״ as ואתגלי ה׳, and ״כי ביום השלישי ירד ה׳״ as יתגלי ה׳. From this it will be understood that the Special Name was revealed at Sinai in His Glory, and it is He who speaks with Moses. And this Glory is El Shaddai, by which the Special Name appeared to the patriarchs; but the Special Name was not made known to them in prophecy, and it is what is called the speculum that does not shine, through which all the prophets without exception gazed." "<b>And you should</b> reflect from this point on: since the special Name is the one that spoke, it would have been fitting for Scripture to say ״וידבר ה׳ את כל הדברים״ (Exodus 20:19), ״אלה״, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 5:22), ״את הדברים האלה דבר ה׳ אל כל קהלכם בהר מתוך האש הענן והערפל קול גדול ולא יסף״, and further it is written (Deuteronomy 5:4), ״וידבר ה׳ אליכם מתוך האש״. But because he mentioned above ״אשר ירד עליו ה׳ באש״, and explained that His great Name is the special Name, which is the attribute of Tiferet, dwelling in fire, therefore it was necessary to mention ״וידבר אלהים״, for ״וידבר אלהים״ is an explanation of ״מתוך האש״, for Israel heard the voice from within the fire. And thus Scripture says (Deuteronomy 5:21), ״הן הראנו ה׳ אלהינו את כבודו ואת גדלו ואת קולו שמענו מתוך האש״, and therefore when he mentioned ״וידבר ה׳ אליכם״ he immediately mentioned ״מתוך האש״, and likewise ״את כל הדברים האלה דבר ה׳״ he immediately mentioned ״מתוך האש״ — for the Hebrew tongue follows the perception of Israel, and therefore he mentioned ״וידבר אלהים״. And the translation of Onkelos followed the perception of Moses, and therefore he translated ״וידבר אלהים״ as ״ומליל ה׳״, and did not translate ״ומליל יקרא דה׳״ or ״אתמלל מן קדם ה׳״ as he translated according to the perception of Israel in the verse ״ואל ידבר עמנו אלהים״ — ״ואל יתמלל עמנא מן קדם ה׳״, for the perception of Moses is beyond the perception of Israel. And this is what is written (Isaiah 26:4), ״כי ביה ה׳״ — he explains that the special Name, which is צור עולמים, in it and through it He speaks and says ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״, and it is within it, as is said (Exodus 23:21), ״כי שמי בקרבו״, and this is the secret of what is written (Exodus 8:18), ״אני ה׳ בקרב הארץ״, by way of the Kabbalah. And He said ״אשר הוצאתיך״, for the bringing out was in the special Name, and this is what is written (Exodus 13:3), ״כי בחזק יד הוציאנו ה׳״, and it is further written ״כי הוציא ה׳״. And He said ״לא יהיה לך אלהים אחרים על פני״ — but My faces shall be your Elohim; and it is like (Genesis 31:50), ״ואם תקח נשים על בנותי״ — but my daughters shall be your wives. From this you will understand (Exodus 33:11), ״ודבר ה׳ אל משה פנים אל פנים״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 34:10), ״אשר ידעו ה׳ פנים אל פנים״. And when He mentioned regarding the people ״פנים בפנים דבר ה׳ עמכם״, therefore He needed to explain ״מתוך האש״ — the power of the Name is beyond the power of a vision within a vision. And this is what is written (Numbers 14:14), ״אשר עין בעין נראה אתה ה׳״, and this is the secret of what is written (below, verse 23), ״לא תעשון אתי״, which is like ״על פני״. And this is what is written (Exodus 19:3), ״ומשה עלה אל האלהים ויקרא אליו ה׳״ — for Moses ascended to the Elohim, and the special Name was speaking within it. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 5), ״ואת קולו שמענו מתוך האש״, for the voice is the voice of the special Name, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 5), ״אם יוספים אנחנו לשמוע את קול ה׳ אלהינו עוד ומתנו״. And this is the secret of what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: זכור and שמור were spoken in one utterance, for זכור is the attribute of Mercy and שמור is the attribute of Judgment, and it is the voice in זכור and שמור — the attribute of Mercy in the attribute of Judgment. And for this reason the tablets were two, five corresponding to five: one tablet corresponding to the attribute of Mercy, and the second tablet, which contains five commandments, likewise in five prohibitions, beginning ״לא תרצח״ corresponding to the attribute of Judgment; and therefore they were two and not all on one tablet. And Moses our master, peace be upon him, who apprehended the special Name, which is the attribute of Tiferet, heard זכור, and all Israel heard שמור. And this is what is written (below, verse 22), ״כי מן השמים דברתי עמכם״ — the intention in it is מתוך השמים, and it is like מתוך האש. And you will not find in any place at the giving of the Torah a statement of the special Name unless it mentions in it מתוך האש." "<b>And behold,</b> the special Name — all Israel heard its voice from within the fire, but they did not see it, even the nobles of the children of Israel, for it is written of it (Exodus 33:20), ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״. And what is written (Exodus 19:11), ״כי ביום השלישי ירד ה׳ לעיני כל העם״ — the intention in this is that when Israel saw this glory as a consuming fire on the top of the mountain, all Israel reflected and recognized that within it was the special Name, which man cannot see, as is written, ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״. But they could see the glory called Elohim, for thus the nobles of the children of Israel saw it, as is said (Exodus 24:10), ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל״, and it is written (Exodus 24:11), ״ויחזו את האלהים״. If so, the meaning of the descent mentioned in the cited passage in this parashah — you should not believe it except of His glory; but the special Name, YHWH, is in heaven, and from there the speech came to the glory that descended on Mount Sinai, and from it it was heard by our ears. And thus it is written (Deuteronomy 4:36), ״מן השמים השמיעך את קולו״, and likewise in Ezra it is written (Nehemiah 9:13), ״ועל הר סיני ירדת ודבר עמהם משמים״." "<b>And you should</b> know that it is the Elohim called Name, and it sits upon the cherubim, in the manner of what is written (2 Samuel 6:2), ״להעלות משם את ארון האלהים אשר נקרא שם שם ה׳ צבאות יושב הכרובים עליו״. And it is He for whom King Solomon, peace be upon him, built the Holy Temple, and therefore he mentions at every turn ״לשמך״ — (1 Kings 8:19), ״והבית אשר בניתי לשמך״, (ibid.), ״הוא יבנה הבית לשמי״ — and to Him is the worship of the sacrifices. And likewise David commanded Solomon, as is said (1 Chronicles 28:9), ״ואתה שלמה בני דע את אלהי אביך ועבדהו״. And you will find written regarding the Levites (Numbers 16:9), ״כי הבדיל אלהי ישראל אתכם״." "<b>And</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote on the verse (Exodus 32), ״כה אמר ה׳ אלהי ישראל שימו איש חרבו על ירכו״ — the worship is to the Elohim of Israel, and there you will find it explicitly. And regarding the sacrifices, it is stated explicitly (2 Chronicles 29), ״ועולה לא העלו בקדש לאלהי ישראל״ — this verse teaches you that the worship of the sacrifices is to the Elohim of Israel. And further explicit verses you will find (Leviticus 21), ״כי את לחם אלהיך הוא מקריב״, and it is written (ibid.), ״לחם אלהיהם הם מקריבים״, and it is written (Leviticus 3), ״לחם אשה״, and it is from the word אש, fire; and likewise (Leviticus 1), ״עולה אשה ריח ניחוח״, like עולת אש. And the reason he did not say לחם אש and mentioned אשה — from the word אשה, meaning 'woman,' that receives — and this is a complete explanation, that the sacrifice belongs to the attribute of Judgment, and therefore he mentioned לחם אשה. And the truth is that the sacrifices are to Elohim, but the intention is to the special Name. And regarding the matter of the intention of prayer — HaShem, blessed be He, is exalted beyond all praise, transcending all laudation, in the manner of what is written, ״ומרומם על כל ברכה ותהלה״, and in the language of the Kaddish: לעילא מכל ברכתא ושירתא תושבחתא." "<b>But</b> the view of the masters of the Kabbalah is that in the One they call Him, and to Him all who praise Him agree — and the intention is upon the special Name, for it is near to all who call upon it. And when? When they call upon it in truth." "<b>And thus</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, mentioned in his piyyut, in the shir 'I shall call — will You not answer?', 'for my shame to ask of the King.'" "<b>And concerning</b> this matter — that among the particulars themselves the verses that speak of it are many and numerous — is what David, of blessed memory, mentioned (Psalms 55): ״אני אל אלהים אקרא וה' יושיעני״, and in the psalm (ibid. 6): ״ה' אל באפך תוכיחני״, he said ״שמע ה' תחנתי ה' תפלתי יקח״. And if you look at Solomon's prayer, you will find that he mentioned in the language of his prayer ״ה' אלהי ישראל״, and did not say merely ״אלהי ישראל״ — for he prayed and said (1 Kings 8): ״ה' אלהי ישראל אין כמוך אלהים בשמים ממעל ועל הארץ מתחת״, and said further (ibid.): ״ה' אלהי ישראל שמור לעבדך דוד אבי את אשר דברת לו״, and ״ועתה ה' אלהי ישראל יאמן נא דברך״; and in all this he informed us that prayer is to the attribute of mercy that is within the attribute of judgment. And likewise he mentioned further: ״והתפללו אל ה' דרך העיר אשר בחרת בה והבית אשר בניתי לשמך״, for the intention of prayer is toward the specific Name, and the building of the House is for the honor of what is called His Name; and he mentioned (ibid.): ״ואתה תשמע השמים״, which is the attribute of mercy, the attribute of Tiferet; and he mentioned at the end of his prayer (ibid.): ״ויהיו דברי אלה אשר התחננתי לפני ה' קרובים אל ה' אלהינו״." "<b>And from now on</b> you will be able to understand with ease the secret of the blessings in the formula of language that the Sages, of blessed memory, established: ״ברוך אתה ה' אלהינו מלך העולם אשר קדשנו במצותיו וצונו״ — and they did not say ״קדשתנו במצותיך וצויתנו״, because the words ״ברוך אתה״ return as a response to ״מלך העולם״, and Tiferet is called עולם, and this world is what has sanctified us and commanded us in His commandments. And so you will find in Sefer ha-Bahir: ״ומאי טעמא אשר קדשנו וצונו ולא אמרינן קדשתנו וצויתנו? מלמד שחי העולמים כלולין בו המצות כולן וברחמיו עלינו נתנם לנו כדי לקדשנו בהם״. And perhaps the reason is ״מאי טעמא כי בשעה שאנו נזכה בעולם הזה לעולם הבא הוא גדול״ — thus far the language of the Bahir. And from here you will understand that the Shekhinah among Israel is not a need of the lowly alone but a need of the High, for since the specific Name is made known through the Glory, the Glory receives an addition of the holy spirit, and this is (Psalms 104): ״יהי כבוד ה' לעולם ישמח ה' במעשיו״. And therefore Israel have the power to weaken or add strength in the supernal Gevurah according to their deeds, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32): ״צור ילדך תשי״, and it is written (Psalms 60): ״באלהים נעשה חיל״. And this Glory is what is mentioned here: ״וידבר אלהים״, and it is the writer and the giver, as it is written (Exodus 32): ״והמכתב מכתב אלהים הוא״, and it is written (ibid. 31): ״ויתן אל משה ככלותו״ — its meaning being ״ויתן אלהים״ at the end of the verse. And it is what Moses our Master called ידיד ה' — ״השוכן לבטח על בנימין״ — for the Temple is in his portion, and with him Israel shall dwell secure from the terror of the enemy; and it is what is called פי ה', for I have already mentioned above that the specific Name is what speaks through it, and it is that concerning which Moses and Aaron sinned in the matter of the Rock; therefore He said (Numbers 20): ״לא האמנתם בי״, ״אשר מריתם את פי״, ״מעלתם בי״ — that is, in My Name; and I shall write about this, with the help of the Name, and from there you will understand the secret of the matter clearly. And it is what is called צור: (Deuteronomy 32) ״הצור תמים פעלו״, (ibid.) ״צור ילדך תשי״, (Exodus 17) ״הנני עומד לפניך שם על הצור״ — and as I shall allude there in the section of Ḥukat ha-Torah when I arrive there, with the help of the Name." "<b>And you should</b> reflect on why it was necessary to say (ibid.): ״והכית בצור״ and not ״והכית בו״. And this Glory is what He said to Jacob (Genesis 31): ״אנכי האל בית אל״, for this Glory is the Elohim of his father, for He said to him (Exodus 3): ״אנכי אלהי אביך אלהי אברהם אלהי יצחק ואלהי יעקב״, and ״ויסתר משה פניו כי ירא מהביט אל האלהים״. And the verse explains to you that this Glory — Beit El — there the specific Name is found, and there it will speak with us. And it is the Good of whom it is written (Exodus 33): ״אני אעביר כל טובי על פניך״, and it is what passes before Moses, as it is said (ibid. 34): ״ויעבור ה' על פניו״ — and there I shall explain, with the help of the Name, and it is what is written (ibid.): ״את פני האדון ה' אלהי ישראל״." "<b>And you should</b> reflect on the words ״את פני האדון״ and ״ויאמר ה' אלהי ישראל״ — that within Him is ה' אלהי ישראל, and therefore he mentioned ״את פני״, which is the secret of ״על פני״; and this is (ibid. 32): ״ויחל משה את פני ה' אלהיו״, for Moses entreated the attribute of judgment. Or ויחל may be as the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained it, from the language of תחלה — and he intended to say that Moses drew strength from the beginning to the end, which is called פנים; and therefore he prayed in the language ״למה ה'״ — that is, why do You give permission to the attribute of judgment to hold sway over Your people. And this Glory is what Moses prayed to at Sinai — it is what is written in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9): ״ואתפלל אל ה' ואומר ה' אלהים אל תשחית עמך ונחלתך אשר פדית בגדלך אשר הוצאת ממצרים ביד חזקה״, and this Name is written with אל\"ף דל\"ת and יו\"ד ה\"א after it, and therefore he entreated before Him and said ״אל תשחית״ — the meaning of the verse being: Adonai, in whom are mercies, do not destroy Your people. And he further mentioned [and said at the bush, 'my Adonai'], (and at Sinai) and said (Deuteronomy 3): ״אדני יהוה אתה החלות״, whose intention is: Adonai, in whom are mercies (You at the bush). And likewise they further mentioned (Exodus 34): ״ילך נא אדני בקרבנו״, and he further said (Numbers 14): ״ועתה יגדל נא כח אדני״, etc. And this Glory is what we bless: ״ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו״, and the meaning of ממקומו is: from the Place from which it receives, which is the attribute of Gevurah, for from there it receives, and from there it is fitting to bless Ein Sof. And after Qedushah we say ברוך, and we distinguish between the language of holiness and the language of blessing because one cannot equate the holiness above with the holiness below, even though the blessing comes from holiness — all is unified. And in the Midrash Tehillim: ״וכי מנין היה יודע משה בהר אימתי יום ואימתי לילה? בזמן שהמלאכים מקלסין להקב״ה בקדוש היה יודע שהוא יום, וכשהיו מקלסין בברוך היה יודע שהוא לילה״; and this is what is written (Hosea 3): ״ובקשו את ה' אלהיהם ואת דויד מלכם״ — and the meaning of this is Tiferet and the Glory; and afterward we say ״ימלוך ה' לעולם אלהיך ציון״, which goes back and joins them as one: ״ימלוך ה' לעולם״ — Tiferet; ״אלהיך ציון״ — the Glory." "<b>And likewise</b> we find in the Aggadah: when the Shekhinah returns to the Holy of Holies, they say, ״ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו״ — 'Blessed be the glory of YHWH from His place.' And the language of the [Midrash] Bahir: 'What is ״ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו״? A parable: a king who had a matronita in his chamber, with whom all his hosts delighted, and they had sons, and they would come every day to see the king's face and bless him. They said to him: Where is our mother? He said to them: You cannot see her. They said: May she be blessed in whatever place she is.' — end quote. And this kavod is what is called Shekhinah, and you already know that it is comprised of six extremities, and behold it is the seventh, pouring power into the six extremities. And likewise Israel heard the Ten Commandments from the mouth of the Shekhinah from within the fire, and from this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: the Torah was given in seven voices, and they are mentioned in this pericope: ״ויהי קלת וברקים וענן״ — it is written קלת, and ״וקול שופר״ — these are two, and it is written ״ויהי קול השופר״ and it is written ״יעננו בקול״ — these are four, and ״וכל העם רואים את הקולת״ — it is written קולת, and ״את קול השופר״ — these are six, and he mentioned first ״וידבר אלהים״ — these are seven. And likewise in the pericope of Va'etḥanan, after the Ten Commandments, you will also find seven voices there; I will write it, with the help of Heaven. And behold, Moses, peace be upon him, attained in this holy assembly seven voices, which are seven sefirot, and they are called in the language of the Kabbalah 'seven sefirot,' and in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory 'seven voices,' and in the language of the Prophets 'seven eyes,' and this is the secret of the verse (Zechariah 3): ״על אבן אחת שבעה עינים״ — 'upon one stone, seven eyes' — from the language of גוונים, hues. And Moses alone is the one who gazes upon the Name and attains them, but Israel, whose attainment did not reach that far, heard the utterance from the mouth of Elohim in a single voice alone — this is what is written (Deuteronomy 5): ״את הדברים האלה דבר ה' אל כל קהלכם״ etc. ״קול גדול ולא יסף״ — 'a great voice and it did not continue' — for the congregation did not hear all the words but only a single voice; they did not attain more than that of them. And so you find with David, peace be upon him, that he mentioned seven voices (Psalms 29): ״קול ה' על המים״, ״קול ה' בכח״, ״קול ה' בהדר״ — these are three, and they are Ḥesed and Gevurah and Tiferet; ״קול ה' שובר ארזים״ — this is Nezah; ״קול ה' חוצב״ — this is Hod; ״קול ה' יחיל מדבר״ — this is Yesod; ״קול ה' יחולל אילות״ — this is Malkhut. And after Moses attained these seven sefirot, he turned back and asked for that which is above them, and that is what he said (Exodus 33): ״הראני נא את כבודך״, and there I shall write, with the help of Heaven." "<b>And you must</b> know that the Shekhinah, which is the seventh, pours power into seventy Names, and the seventy Names into the seventy nations over which they are appointed. And when the utterance went forth from the mouth of the Shekhinah, it was divided into seven voices, and from seven voices into seventy languages; from this every single nation heard the utterance, for it was divided into seventy languages." "<b>And likewise</b> they said in the Midrash on Psalms (Psalms 68): ״ה' יתן אומר המבשרות צבא רב״ — 'HaShem gives the utterance; the heralds are a great host' — the utterance went forth and was divided into seven voices, and from seven voices into seventy languages, like a man striking the anvil and sparks flying this way and that; so too ״המבשרות צבא רב״. From here you will understand the secret of the oath (שבועה) and the vow (נדר), for the term שבועה is derived from שבע, seven, and the seven sefirot; whereas the vow is above the oath. And because the Torah's domain is only among the seven mentioned, and the Torah came forth from there alone, the oath cannot take effect to annul a commandment — for it is impossible for a thing to rise above its own measure, so how could the commandment be annulled because of the oath? For the root of the commandment is from the same root as the oath. But the vow, which is above the oath, does take effect to annul the commandment, because its root rises above the root of the oath, and therefore it has the power to annul the commandment, which derives from the oath that is below it. And the very terms נדר and שבועה testify about them and indicate their nature: for the vow (נדר) has no fixed limit, and its expression is ״הרי הוא עלי״ — 'it is upon me' — an expression indicating that which has no fixed limit; and a proof-text for this (Psalms 56): ״עלי אלהים נדריך״, and likewise (Numbers 30): ״ונדריה עליה״. But the oath (שבועה) does have a fixed limit, for the count of seven is a fixed thing. And in the pericope of לא תשא — 'You shall not take' — I shall explain the secret of the oath and the reason for the severity of the prohibition within it, and in the pericope of the heads of the tribes (Numbers 30) I shall explain the secret of the vow, with the help of Heaven. Behold, I have explained to you in this verse some of the roots of the Torah; I shall not need to elaborate upon them in other places, for from here you will be able to understand them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"], 3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {5 "The comment cites Exodus 20:19 as a prooftext in its first sentence, but the verse numbering in the Hebrew parenthetical (ה׳:י״ט = 5:19) appears corrupt; the verse content cited matches Exodus 20:19. Left as written per the exact-numbers rule.", 6 "Citations Exodus 19:11 and 24:10–11 given without explicit verse numbers in the Hebrew; verse numbers supplied from content.", 7 "The citation of Numbers is given as ט״ז:ט׳ (16:9) in the Hebrew; the verse 'כי הבדיל אלהי ישראל אתכם' occurs at Numbers 16:9. Translated as written.", 8 "The citations from Leviticus in the Hebrew give chapter numbers only (כא, ג, א) without verse numbers; rendered as written.", 14 "The bracketed text [ואמר בסנה בי אדני] and parentheticals (ובסיני) and (אתה בסנה) appear to be marginal glosses or alternate readings embedded in the body; translated as they stand."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:12.641299279Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}